WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.440 --> 00:00:03.260
What do you think of when you think about Ninjas in Pyjamas?

00:00:03.260 --> 00:00:08.560
Beyond the obvious thoughts you have when you first hear that ridiculous phrase, CS:GO fans probably

00:00:08.560 --> 00:00:11.440
think about an eternally underachieving team from Sweden.

00:00:11.540 --> 00:00:15.500
"There will be no Major in 2017 for NiP."

00:00:15.500 --> 00:00:17.940
"Aren't going to be attending the Minor, they've been eliminated."

00:00:18.040 --> 00:00:20.760
"NiP not making it the Minor once again."

00:00:20.800 --> 00:00:25.200
The team once cursed by a Bay Area witch to only win tournaments in Oakland.

00:00:25.400 --> 00:00:29.740
But once upon a time, NiP were the best CS:GO team in the world.

00:00:30.100 --> 00:00:34.280
Once upon a time, NiP won 87 maps in a row.

00:00:38.940 --> 00:00:45.160
87 LAN maps in a row is one of the most impressive esports records of all time.

00:00:45.160 --> 00:00:48.700
And no team in CS:GO has really matched it since.

00:00:48.740 --> 00:00:54.120
"It was just very cool and surprising to see that you can actually be so dominant in a game and also that

00:00:54.120 --> 00:00:56.180
you were one of the best player in the same time."

00:00:56.740 --> 00:00:58.320
"That's probably my biggest success."

00:00:58.400 --> 00:01:00.220
It actually just seems impossible.

00:01:00.520 --> 00:01:05.620
Who could possibly win 87 LAN maps without dropping a single one.

00:01:05.620 --> 00:01:10.360
For comparison, Cloud9, the Boston Major champions, played 16 maps that tournament.

00:01:10.660 --> 00:01:16.440
And they dropped four of them. Most teams, that win tournaments drop a few maps here and there,

00:01:16.760 --> 00:01:21.040
When was the last time you saw a CS:GO team win a tournament without dropping a map?

00:01:21.300 --> 00:01:22.600
Now imagine winning ten.

00:01:23.300 --> 00:01:28.720
Between August 2012, when they won SteelSeries GO, and April 2013, when they placed second

00:01:28.720 --> 00:01:34.840
at StarLadder StarSeries V. NiP played 10 LAN tournaments, and didn't drop a single map.

00:01:35.060 --> 00:01:39.220
That means that NiP dominated the earliest era of competitive CS:GO.

00:01:39.300 --> 00:01:43.860
The game came out in August 2012, the same week NiP won their first tournament.

00:01:43.860 --> 00:01:46.720
NiP were the first legendary CS:GO team.

00:01:46.780 --> 00:01:49.540
According to esports journalist Duncan “Thorin” Shields,

00:01:49.680 --> 00:01:53.280
NiP had a lot going for them at the time that made that streak possible.

00:01:53.600 --> 00:01:58.740
For one, some of the best Counter-Strike 1.6 teams in the world had yet to transition to CS:GO.

00:01:59.040 --> 00:02:05.020
"Part of the reason as to why these teams didn't change lineups and why they came over so late to CS:GO"

00:02:05.300 --> 00:02:10.220
"is 'cause 1.6 didn't actually die when NiP had begun playing CS:GO."

00:02:10.220 --> 00:02:16.160
"When they won that first tournament in CS:GO in August of 2012, there was still a number of months and number

00:02:16.160 --> 00:02:21.860
of 1.6 big sized tournaments with higher prize purses at times for first place than CS:GO."

00:02:21.920 --> 00:02:23.940
CS:GO had a messy launch.

00:02:24.400 --> 00:02:27.380
Part of it was that the game was still, unbalanced and unrefined.

00:02:27.420 --> 00:02:33.040
But another part of it was that dedicated CS 1.6 players had poured thousands of hours into their game

00:02:40.220 --> 00:02:43.240
CS:GO just wasn’t 1.6.

00:02:44.620 --> 00:02:49.880
So, for a while, a bunch of pro teams just… kept playing 1.6.

00:02:50.280 --> 00:02:54.740
There were concurrent competitive communities for both CS:GO and 1.6

00:02:54.980 --> 00:03:00.520
and that meant some of the best Counter-Strike players in the world just weren’t at CS:GO tournaments.

00:03:00.840 --> 00:03:05.540
ESC didn’t enter CS:GO until September 2012, and were slow to make roster

00:03:05.560 --> 00:03:09.640
changes that they would need to become the dominant force they would be known as as Virtus.pro.

00:03:09.980 --> 00:03:15.600
Fnatic and Na'Vi took longer to enter CS:GO, but they wouldn't pose a serious threat for months.

00:03:15.880 --> 00:03:19.220
NiP on the other hand, was going to tournaments from day one.

00:03:19.340 --> 00:03:23.720
They were there at the beginning. They had more time with the game and more time on the maps.

00:03:23.800 --> 00:03:26.640
And that’s an advantage that can only be mitigated by time.

00:03:26.660 --> 00:03:30.320
But that number, 87-0, kind of defies all explanation.

00:03:30.320 --> 00:03:34.540
Some team had to have put in the effort to beat NiP on just one map.

00:03:34.680 --> 00:03:37.100
Some team had to have found the crack in their armor.

00:03:37.760 --> 00:03:38.480
But they didn't.

00:03:38.960 --> 00:03:43.120
The only team that actually held a candle to NiP was VeryGames, the French troster that

00:03:43.120 --> 00:03:44.540
went on to become Titan.

00:03:44.740 --> 00:03:49.940
Unfortunately back then, CS:GO had a limited map pool  and that made it even harder to beat NiP.

00:03:50.040 --> 00:03:55.280
VeryGames chose to focus on four maps. But NiP were the best at all five maps.

00:03:55.360 --> 00:04:15.400
(Casting)

00:04:15.400 --> 00:04:21.040
NiP’s reign finally came to an end at StarLadder Star Series V, where they lost to Virtus.Pro.

00:04:21.040 --> 00:04:25.000
That was an all-CIS roster featuring the likes of ANGE1 and Dosia,

00:04:25.000 --> 00:04:53.400
(Casting)

00:04:53.400 --> 00:04:59.100
"That felt quite sad when it happened, but it was like, it was still such an amazing run I mean I could never

00:04:59.100 --> 00:05:00.960
believe that anything like that would happen."

00:05:00.960 --> 00:05:07.200
It was a sign that other teams had begun to figure out CS:GO, or at the very least, figure out NiP.

00:05:07.300 --> 00:05:12.080
(Casting)

00:05:12.080 --> 00:05:17.800
(Casting)

00:05:17.800 --> 00:05:21.180
The record stands as one of the most impressive in esports history.

00:05:21.220 --> 00:05:26.240
And while NiP doesn’t really live up to it these days, you have to respect a team built on the foundation

00:05:26.240 --> 00:05:30.240
of the once-undisputed CS:GO best team in the world.

